      <h2>AGI Publications</h2>
      <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=pZmOvyYAAAAJ" class="view-all">View full publication at Google Scholar →</a>

      <!-- Working Papers -->
      <h3 class="pub-subsection">Working Papers & Preprints</h3>
      <ul class="pub-list">

        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue-tag">June 2026</span>
          <div class="pub-title">Mnemosyne: Agentic Transaction Processing for Validating and Repairing AI-generated Workflows</div>
          <div class="pub-authors">Edward Y. Chang, Longling Geng, and Emily J. Chang</div>
          <div class="pub-tldr"><strong>TL;DR: </strong>To protect transactional integrity from unreliable generative planners, Agentic Transaction Processing (ATP) treats all model actions as untrusted proposals that must pass deterministic runtime admission under an executable constraint set, a model realized by the open-source Mnemosyne runtime to decouple committed-state correctness from the intelligence layer with minimal computational overhead.</div>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.00269">arXiv</a>
            <a href="ArchitecturalWisdom.bib">BibTeX</a>
          </div>
        </li>

      <ul class="pub-list">
        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue-tag">June 2026</span>
          <div class="pub-title">Architectural Wisdom: A Framework for Governing Optimization in AI Systems</div>
          <div class="pub-authors">Edward Y. Chang</div>
          <div class="pub-tldr"><strong>TL;DR: </strong>A framework for governing what AI systems optimize: a wisdom layer that constrains what an agent should optimize, restrain, and preserve, rather than only how well it optimizes. Foundational to <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/book/10.1145/3749421">The Path to AGI, Volume III</a>.</div>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.16319">arXiv</a>
            <a href="ArchitecturalWisdom.bib">BibTeX</a>
          </div>
        </li>

      <ul class="pub-list">
        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue-tag">May 2026</span>
          <div class="pub-title">Trivium: Temporal Regret as a First-Class Objective for Causal-Memory Controllers</div>
          <div class="pub-authors">Edward Y. Chang</div>
          <div class="pub-tldr"><strong>TL;DR: </strong>Promotes long-horizon temporal regret to a first-class objective alongside outcome regret: outcome-only correction captures only the <em>what</em> of failure, while epistemic and temporal regret add the <em>why</em> and the <em>when</em>, yielding logarithmic delayed-identification regret over a persistent causal transaction log. </div>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04421">arXiv</a>
            <a href="Trivium.bib">BibTeX</a>
          </div>
        </li>


      <ul class="pub-list">
        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue-tag">March-April 2026</span>
          <div class="pub-title">Exploring Collatz Dynamics with Human–LLM Collaboration</div>
          <div class="pub-authors">Edward Y. Chang</div>
          <div class="pub-tldr"><strong>TL;DR: </strong>We prove that Collatz burst–gap dynamics exhibit geometric distributions in a modular model, yielding a conditional convergence framework that reduces the conjecture to a single orbit equidistribution hypothesis — developed and stress-tested through a documented human–LLM collaboration whose failure modes are themselves analyzed. </div>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11066">arXiv</a>
            <a href="CausalT5K.bib">BibTeX</a>
          </div>
        </li>

      <ul class="pub-list">
        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue-tag">February 2026</span>
          <div class="pub-title">Epistemic Regret Minimization: Label-Free Causal Critique Beyond Outcome Reward</div>
          <div class="pub-authors">Edward Y. Chang, Longling Geng</div>
          <div class="pub-tldr"><strong>TL;DR:</strong>ERM critiques how LLMs reason causally, not just what they answer, catching shortcuts that outcome-only reward entrenches, without requiring ground-truth labels.</div>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11675">arXiv</a>
            <a href="RAudit.bib">BibTeX</a>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue-tag">arXiv 2025</span>
          <div class="pub-title">UCCT: The Unified Cognitive Consciousness Theory for Language Models: Anchoring Semantics, Thresholds of Activation, and Emergent Reasoning</div>
          <div class="pub-authors">Edward Y. Chang, Zeyneb N. Kaya, Ethan Chang</div>
          <div class="pub-tldr"><strong>TL;DR:</strong> A unified theory explaining how LLMs turn pretrained capacity into goal-directed behavior via semantic anchoring. Formalizes anchoring strength S = ρ<sub>d</sub> − d<sub>r</sub> − log k, predicting threshold-like performance flips and generalizing ICL, retrieval, and fine-tuning as anchoring variants.</div>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.02139">arXiv</a>
            <a href="UCCT.bib">BibTeX</a>
          </div>
        </li>

        <li class="pub-item">
          <span class="pub-venue-tag">arXiv 2024</span>
          <div class="pub-title">EVINCE: Optimizing Adversarial LLM Dialogues via Conditional Statistics and Information Theory</div>
          <div class="pub-authors">Edward Y. Chang</div>
          <div class="pub-tldr"><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Uses information-theoretic metrics to optimize multi-agent debates, measuring when additional dialogue rounds yield diminishing returns.</div>
          <div class="pub-links">
            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14575">arXiv</a>
            <a href="EVINCE2024.bib">BibTeX</a>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>

